WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:24.278
Blood is a useful substance, not just for
our life, but for our way of thinking.

00:00:24.278 --> 00:00:31.160
It's served as a convenient metaphor for
so many ideas, covering the extremes and even

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.318
the contradictions of our experiences.

00:00:34.318 --> 00:00:39.046
It signifies life, but also accompanies death.

00:00:39.046 --> 00:00:44.414
It unites those who share it, but in doing
so it divides others.

00:00:44.414 --> 00:00:47.196
It runs hot, it runs cold.

00:00:47.196 --> 00:00:55.305
Whatever it is we need to describe, blood is 
there for us to project onto, flowing through us.

00:00:55.305 --> 00:01:01.059
And yet under the microscope, blood doesn’t
seem that remarkable.

00:01:01.059 --> 00:01:08.230
The colors may be vivid, but the cells are
so simple and similar to one another.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:12.817
How is it that our complicated, 
contradictory sense of self,

00:01:12.817 --> 00:01:16.850
 recorded in literature and painting and film…

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:24.680
how does all that come down to a fluid made
up largely of the simplest cell in our body—

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:31.409
a cell so singularly focused on its task of
carrying hemoglobin that it actively has to

00:01:31.409 --> 00:01:35.849
get rid of the organelles and DNA inside of
it?

00:01:35.849 --> 00:01:40.970
But if we track the origin of these cells,
we get a more complicated picture of what

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:46.314
they mean to us and to our bodies, and even
to our own identity.

00:01:51.557 --> 00:01:57.590
Our blood is a soup made up of cells swimming
around in water, salts and proteins.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:04.107
There are platelets, tiny fragments of cells
that help clot our blood when a vessel breaks.

00:02:04.107 --> 00:02:09.359
Then there are white blood cells, which help
protect our body from infection.

00:02:09.359 --> 00:02:14.392
But the cells that are the most prevalent
in our blood are red blood cells.

00:02:14.392 --> 00:02:19.440
They’re pigmented red by hemoglobin, which
helps the cells transport oxygen to our tissues

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.586
and organs, and then transports carbon dioxide
back to the lungs.

00:02:24.586 --> 00:02:29.630
The blood you’re seeing now came from James,
our master of microscopes, who had just been

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.959
scratched by his cats.

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:38.145
So you can see the red blood cells that came
from the wound, along with the white blood cells.

00:02:38.145 --> 00:02:44.056
And to better see those white blood cells,
James added something else to his slide:

00:02:44.056 --> 00:02:45.313
spit.

00:02:45.313 --> 00:02:50.600
That spit carried bacteria, fresh targets
for the white blood cells.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:55.150
In our bodies, white blood cells have a very
specific job:

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:59.150
they decide what belongs there, and what does
not.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:04.430
They seek out molecular markers on bacteria
and other microbes that have become familiar

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:08.220
to our bodies over ages of evolution.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:14.080
There are different types of white blood cells,
and they vary in the types of organisms they

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.400
look for and how they deal with them.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.599
But our favorites might be the white blood
cells that, like the one in the middle of

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:26.085
our screen here, consume their target.

00:03:26.085 --> 00:03:33.959
Immunity is often described in war-like terms,
but I like to think of it as something else:

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:35.290
identity.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:41.430
In effect, the white blood cells are part
of an elaborate mechanism that decides what

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.580
is our body, and what is not.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:50.819
They shape our identity from within, like
a bouncer who decides on the vibe of a nightclub

00:03:50.819 --> 00:03:53.447
based on who they let pass.

00:03:53.447 --> 00:03:59.319
But bouncers occasionally make mistakes, and
so do white blood cells.

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:04.680
The consequences for white blood cells are
usually more dire, especially if they begin

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:10.150
to turn on us and attack our own bodies, leading
to autoimmune disorders.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:15.878
Their task of deciding what belongs to us
and what doesn’t is a precarious one,

00:04:15.878 --> 00:04:21.053
a task that still requires outside input as
we attempt to shape their responses with

00:04:21.053 --> 00:04:23.471
medication or treatment.

00:04:23.471 --> 00:04:28.850
In comparison, the tasks of red blood cells
are much more straightforward.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:33.110
They carry oxygen and carbon dioxide throughout
our body.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:39.100
But despite their very different lives, the
origins of platelets, red blood cells, and

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:41.764
white blood cells have a significant overlap.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:43.446
They are all—

00:04:43.446 --> 00:04:46.020
with the exception of a few white blood cells—

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.760
made in the bone marrow, where they start
as myeloid stem cells.

00:04:53.196 --> 00:05:00.040
We describe stem cells as young and immature,
but what we really mean is that they have potential—

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:05.154
the potential to take on one of a number of
identities once they begin maturing.

00:05:05.154 --> 00:05:11.550
As the cells divide, signals that describe
what the body needs shape what they become.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:15.898
If we are low on oxygen, the bone marrow makes
red blood cells.

00:05:15.898 --> 00:05:19.154
If we're sick, it makes white blood cells.

00:05:19.154 --> 00:05:23.800
And these identities require distinct processes
to make them possible.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:29.420
For example, to make themselves capable of
holding more oxygen, red blood cells get rid

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:35.220
of their nucleus and other organelles as they
mature, expelling them for white blood cells

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.166
called macrophages to clear up.

00:05:38.166 --> 00:05:45.296
Consider that a healthy human adult is producing
2 million red blood cells every second.

00:05:45.296 --> 00:05:50.298
Now imagine 2 million nuclei being spat out
throughout your body.

00:05:50.298 --> 00:05:55.270
And it falls on our macrophages to clear them
out, to ensure that these bits of us that

00:05:55.270 --> 00:06:00.347
were definitely once us, now no longer are.

00:06:00.347 --> 00:06:06.856
That simplification makes the red blood cells
so capable at their singular task.

00:06:06.856 --> 00:06:09.995
But what if that wasn’t their only job?

00:06:09.995 --> 00:06:12.126
What if they could do more?

00:06:12.126 --> 00:06:13.957
Well, they might.

00:06:13.957 --> 00:06:19.210
Scientists recently reported finding evidence
that red blood cells might work alongside

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:25.810
white blood cells and the rest of our immune
system, using receptors to find signs of infection

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:31.947
or tissue damage, and to signal that to the
rest of the immune system so it can act.

00:06:31.947 --> 00:06:36.930
They may even sacrifice themselves to macrophages
to trigger the alarm.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:42.340
This work is relatively new though, and there
is still a lot to learn about the role red

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.722
blood cells play in immunity.

00:06:44.722 --> 00:06:50.870
And the more we learn about blood, the more
malleable our metaphors and the more complicated

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:55.150
the interplay between identity and immunity
become.

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:58.727
But of course, identity is dynamic.

00:06:58.727 --> 00:07:02.021
How can it not be, when it flows through our
veins?

00:07:05.546 --> 00:07:11.750
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:17.099
The names on the screen are our Patreon patrons,
these are the people who have decided to directly

00:07:17.099 --> 00:07:24.131
support this channel over at patreon.com/journeytomicro,
so we always want to take this time at the

00:07:24.131 --> 00:07:27.430
end of our videos to thank them for their
support.

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:31.699
If you want to see more from our master of
